{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"The React team started work on React 18, which will be its next major version.","source_span":"We’ve started work on the React 18 release, which will be our next major version."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"The React team created a Working Group to prepare the community for gradual adoption of new features in React 18.","source_span":"We’ve created a Working Group to prepare the community for gradual adoption of new features in React 18."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"The React team published a React 18 Alpha so library authors could try it and give feedback.","source_span":"We’ve published a React 18 Alpha so that library authors can try it and provide feedback."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"React 18 will include automatic batching, a new startTransition API, and a new streaming server renderer with built-in support for React.lazy.","source_span":"out-of-the-box improvements (like automatic batching), new APIs (like startTransition), and a new streaming server renderer with built-in support for React.lazy"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"The new opt-in mechanism in React 18 is called \"concurrent rendering\" and lets React prepare multiple versions of the UI at the same time.","source_span":"It’s called “concurrent rendering” and it lets React prepare multiple versions of the UI at the same time."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"Concurrent rendering is only enabled for updates triggered by one of the new features, instead of an all-or-nothing \"mode\".","source_span":"concurrent rendering will only be enabled for updates triggered by one of the new features"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"Developers will be able to adopt React 18 without rewrites and try the new features at their own pace.","source_span":"you will be able to adopt React 18 without rewrites and try the new features at your own pace"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"The React team expects many users will be able to upgrade to React 18 within a single afternoon.","source_span":"we expect that many users will be able to upgrade within a single afternoon"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"Facebook successfully shipped concurrent features to tens of thousands of components.","source_span":"We successfully shipped concurrent features to tens of thousands of components at Facebook"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"The React 18 Working Group is hosted on GitHub Discussions and is available for the public to read.","source_span":"The Working Group is hosted on GitHub Discussions and is available for the public to read."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"Only invited members can create or comment on Working Group threads, but the threads are fully visible to the public.","source_span":"only invited members will be allowed to create or comment on threads. However, the threads are fully visible to the public"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"New React 18 alphas are regularly published to npm using the @alpha tag, built from the most recent commit to the main repo.","source_span":"New alphas are regularly published to npm using the `@alpha` tag. These releases are built using the most recent commit to our main repo."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"When a feature or bugfix is merged, it appears in an alpha release the following weekday.","source_span":"When a feature or bugfix is merged, it will appear in an alpha the following weekday."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"Alpha releases of React 18 are not recommended for user-facing, production applications.","source_span":"alpha releases are not recommended for user-facing, production applications"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","statement":"The projected React 18 release timeline is: Library Alpha available immediately, Public Beta at least several months later, Release Candidate (RC) at least several weeks after Beta, and General Availability at least several weeks after RC.","source_span":"Library Alpha: Available today * Public Beta: At least several months * Release Candidate (RC): At least several weeks after Beta * General Availability: At least several weeks after RC"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","fact_refs":[4,5],"question":"What is \"concurrent rendering\" in React 18 and under what condition is it enabled?","answer":"Concurrent rendering is a new opt-in mechanism that lets React prepare multiple versions of the UI at the same time. It is only enabled for updates triggered by one of the new React 18 features, rather than being an all-or-nothing mode."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","fact_refs":[8],"question":"According to the React 18 plan announcement, how many components at Facebook had concurrent features successfully shipped to them?","answer":"Concurrent features were successfully shipped to tens of thousands of components at Facebook."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","fact_refs":[9,10],"question":"Who can create or comment on threads in the React 18 Working Group, and can the public still see the discussions?","answer":"Only invited members are allowed to create or comment on threads in the React 18 Working Group. However, the threads are fully visible to the public, since the group is hosted on GitHub Discussions."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","fact_refs":[11,12,13],"question":"How often are new React 18 alpha releases published, and are they recommended for production apps?","answer":"New React 18 alphas are regularly published to npm using the @alpha tag, and a merged feature or bugfix appears in an alpha the following weekday. Alpha releases are not recommended for user-facing, production applications."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","fact_refs":[14],"question":"What are the stages of the projected React 18 release timeline after the Library Alpha?","answer":"After the Library Alpha (available immediately), the timeline is: Public Beta at least several months later, Release Candidate (RC) at least several weeks after Beta, and General Availability at least several weeks after RC."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_the-plan-for-react-18","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What new features does the \"Plan for React 18\" post say React 18 will include?","answer":"React 18 will include out-of-the-box improvements like automatic batching, new APIs like startTransition, and a new streaming server renderer with built-in support for React.lazy."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-conf-2021-recap","statement":"The React team hosted its 6th React Conf the week before this December 17, 2021 recap post.","source_span":"Last week we hosted our 6th React Conf."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-conf-2021-recap","statement":"React Conf 2021 was the first time React Conf was hosted online, and it was streamed for free and translated into 8 different languages.","source_span":"This was the first time React Conf was hosted online, and it was streamed for free, translated to 8 different languages."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-conf-2021-recap","statement":"Over 50,000 people registered for React Conf 2021.","source_span":"Over 50,000 people registered"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-conf-2021-recap","statement":"There were over 60,000 views of the 19 talks at React Conf 2021.","source_span":"with over 60,000 views of 19 talks"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-conf-2021-recap","statement":"5,000 participants joined Discord across both the conference and replay events.","source_span":"5,000 participants in Discord across both events"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-conf-2021-recap","statement":"React 18 adds the long-awaited concurrent renderer and updates to Suspense without any major breaking changes.","source_span":"React 18 adds the long-awaited concurrent renderer and updates to Suspense without any major breaking changes."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-conf-2021-recap","statement":"There is no \"concurrent mode\" in React 18, only concurrent features.","source_span":"This means there is no concurrent mode, only concurrent features."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-conf-2021-recap","statement":"At the keynote, the React team announced that the React 18 Release Candidate (RC) is available to try.","source_span":"we also announced that the React 18 RC is available to try now"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-conf-2021-recap","statement":"To try the React 18 RC, developers run npm install react@rc react-dom@rc.","source_span":"npm install react@rc react-dom@rc"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-conf-2021-recap","statement":"The first React Working Group, formed for React 18, helped create the gradual adoption strategy and refine new APIs such as useId, useSyncExternalStore, and useInsertionEffect.","source_span":"Together we worked to create our gradual adoption strategy and refine new APIs such as `useId`, `useSyncExternalStore`, and `useInsertionEffect`."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-conf-2021-recap","statement":"The React team announced a newly formed React DevTools team and a new Timeline Profiler to help developers debug their React apps.","source_span":"we also announced the newly formed React DevTools team and a new Timeline Profiler to help developers debug their React apps"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-conf-2021-recap","fact_refs":[2,3],"question":"How many people registered for React Conf 2021, and how many views did its talks receive?","answer":"Over 50,000 people registered for React Conf 2021, and its 19 talks received over 60,000 views."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-conf-2021-recap","fact_refs":[1],"question":"Was React Conf 2021 the first time the conference was hosted online, and how many languages was it translated into?","answer":"Yes, React Conf 2021 was the first time it was hosted online, and it was streamed for free and translated into 8 different languages."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-conf-2021-recap","fact_refs":[6],"question":"Does React 18 have a \"concurrent mode\" according to the React Conf 2021 recap?","answer":"No — the recap states there is no concurrent mode in React 18, only concurrent features."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-conf-2021-recap","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What command do you run to try the React 18 Release Candidate that was announced at React Conf 2021?","answer":"You run `npm install react@rc react-dom@rc` to install the React 18 Release Candidate."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-conf-2021-recap","fact_refs":[9],"question":"Which new APIs did the first React Working Group help refine for React 18?","answer":"The Working Group helped refine useId, useSyncExternalStore, and useInsertionEffect, along with creating the gradual adoption strategy."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-conf-2021-recap","fact_refs":[10],"question":"What developer tooling announcements were made at React Conf 2021?","answer":"The React team announced a newly formed React DevTools team and a new Timeline Profiler to help developers debug their React apps."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","statement":"React 18 will ship in a future version of React Native because it relies on the New React Native Architecture.","source_span":"React 18 will ship in a future version of React Native. This is because React 18 relies on the New React Native Architecture"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","statement":"To install the latest version of React, run npm install react react-dom.","source_span":"npm install react react-dom"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","statement":"In React 18, ReactDOM.render is no longer supported and createRoot should be used instead.","source_span":"ReactDOM.render is no longer supported in React 18. Use createRoot instead."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","statement":"unmountComponentAtNode was changed to root.unmount in React 18.","source_span":"We’ve also changed `unmountComponentAtNode` to `root.unmount`"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","statement":"The callback argument to render was removed because it usually does not have the expected result when using Suspense.","source_span":"We've also removed the callback from render, since it usually does not have the expected result when using Suspense."}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","statement":"Apps using server-side rendering with hydration should upgrade from hydrate to hydrateRoot.","source_span":"if your app uses server-side rendering with hydration, upgrade `hydrate` to `hydrateRoot`"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","statement":"renderToNodeStream is deprecated in React 18.","source_span":"`renderToNodeStream`: **Deprecated ⛔️️**"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","statement":"renderToPipeableStream is the new API to use for streaming in Node environments.","source_span":"Instead, for streaming in Node environments, use: * `renderToPipeableStream`: **New ✨**"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","statement":"renderToReadableStream is a new API supporting streaming SSR with Suspense for edge runtime environments such as Deno and Cloudflare Workers.","source_span":"introducing a new API to support streaming SSR with Suspense for modern edge runtime environments, such as Deno and Cloudflare workers: * `renderToReadableStream`: **New ✨**"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","statement":"renderToString and renderToStaticMarkup continue to work in React 18 but have limited support for Suspense.","source_span":"The following APIs will continue working, but with limited support for Suspense: * `renderToString`: **Limited** ⚠️ * `renderToStaticMarkup`: **Limited** ⚠️"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","statement":"renderToStaticNodeStream continues to work in React 18 for rendering e-mails.","source_span":"this API will continue to work for rendering e-mails: * `renderToStaticNodeStream`"}
{"type":"fact","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","statement":"With the updated TypeScript types for React 18, the children prop must now be listed explicitly when defining props.","source_span":"The most notable change is that the `children` prop now needs to be listed explicitly when defining props"}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","fact_refs":[0],"question":"Why won't React 18 be immediately available to React Native users?","answer":"React 18 relies on the New React Native Architecture, so it will only ship in a future version of React Native."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","fact_refs":[2],"question":"What API should be used instead of ReactDOM.render in React 18?","answer":"createRoot should be used instead — ReactDOM.render is no longer supported in React 18."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","fact_refs":[3],"question":"What did unmountComponentAtNode get renamed to in React 18?","answer":"unmountComponentAtNode was changed to root.unmount."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","fact_refs":[6,7],"question":"Which server rendering API is deprecated in React 18, and what should be used instead for streaming in Node environments?","answer":"renderToNodeStream is deprecated in React 18; for streaming in Node environments, renderToPipeableStream should be used instead."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","fact_refs":[8],"question":"What new React 18 API supports streaming SSR with Suspense in edge runtimes like Deno and Cloudflare Workers?","answer":"renderToReadableStream is the new API introduced for streaming SSR with Suspense in edge runtime environments such as Deno and Cloudflare Workers."}
{"type":"seed","doc_id":"d3_react_react-18-upgrade-guide","fact_refs":[11],"question":"What TypeScript typing change was introduced for the children prop in React 18?","answer":"With the updated React 18 TypeScript types, the children prop now needs to be listed explicitly when defining component props."}
